# ============================================================================
# PAUSED — this file is INERT at this filename.
#
# Dependabot reads `.github/dependabot.yml` exactly. Renamed, it does nothing.
#
# WHY. Ten workspaces means every update round opens work in ten places, and
# every pull request runs the full nine-job CI matrix, and every PR event also
# wakes the CLA workflow. Measured on 2026-09-18, its first day on:
#
#     122 of the day's 322 workflow runs in this repository were dependabot's,
#     and the day's total was ~2,256 billable runner-minutes (~$18) on a
#     PRIVATE repository, where minutes are charged.
#
# The updater is not wrong and the configuration below is the corrected,
# grouped one. The economics are wrong while the repository is private.
#
# RE-ENABLE by renaming this file back to `.github/dependabot.yml` AFTER the
# repository is public, where Actions minutes on standard runners are free.
# Until then, `scripts/dependency_policy.sh` is what actually holds the line on
# dependencies, and it runs in CI on every push at no extra cost.
# ============================================================================

# SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later
# Dependency updates.
#
# One `cargo` entry per WORKSPACE, not one for the repository. There are ten,
# each committing its own Cargo.lock, and an update to one does not touch the
# others. A single root entry would have left nine unwatched -- which is how
# RUSTSEC-2026-0285 (rustls) came to sit in three lockfiles at once while
# everything looked green.
#
# VOLUME IS THE WHOLE DESIGN PROBLEM. Ten workspaces share most of their
# dependency graph, so an ungrouped configuration opens the SAME bump ten times.
# Measured: the first configuration here used the default one-PR-per-dependency
# behaviour with a limit of 5 and immediately opened 48 pull requests. A review
# queue nobody can read is indistinguishable from no updates at all, and on a
# public repository it is the first thing a visitor sees.
#
# So: ONE grouped PR per workspace covering every update type, limit 2. A
# security advisory still arrives on its own regardless of grouping -- that is
# Dependabot's behaviour for security updates and it is the case that must never
# be buried.
#
# Keep this list in step with `scripts/dependency_policy.sh`, which derives the
# same set from `git ls-files '*Cargo.lock'`.
#
# cla.yml already allowlists dependabot[bot], so these PRs are not CLA-blocked.
version: 2
updates:
  - package-ecosystem: cargo
    directory: "/"
    schedule:
      interval: weekly
      day: monday
    open-pull-requests-limit: 2
    labels: ["dependencies"]
    groups:
      all-dependencies:
        patterns: ["*"]
        update-types: ["minor", "patch", "major"]
  - package-ecosystem: cargo
    directory: "/crates/blut-dsl"
